<p>Here is a number worth paying attention to: SMS messages have an open rate of around 98%. Compare that to email, which hovers between 20% and 30% on a good day. Text messages get read. Almost all of them. Almost immediately.</p>

<p>For small businesses, that kind of attention is gold. You are not competing with a crowded inbox or battling an algorithm. You are landing directly in someone's pocket, and they are reading your message within minutes of receiving it.</p>

<p>But that power comes with responsibility. Get SMS marketing right and it becomes one of the most effective channels in your toolkit. Get it wrong and you will annoy people, damage your <a href="/blog/reputation-management">reputation</a>, and potentially break the law. This guide covers both sides.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Why SMS works so well for small business</h2>

<p>The strength of SMS is immediacy. When you send a text message, it gets seen fast. The average response time for a text is 90 seconds. For email, it is 90 minutes. That difference matters when you are sending appointment reminders, time-sensitive offers, or follow-ups that need a quick response.</p>

<p>SMS also feels personal. A text from a business feels more like a message from someone you know than a marketing blast. That intimacy is why response rates are so much higher. People interact with texts differently than they interact with emails or social posts.</p>

<p>For local and service-based businesses in particular, SMS fills a gap that other channels cannot. It is perfect for the kind of short, time-sensitive, action-oriented communication that makes up most of your customer interactions.</p>

<blockquote><p>Email is where you educate. SMS is where you activate. Use both, but know which job each one does best.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Staying compliant: the Australian rules</h2>

<p>Before you send a single text, you need to understand the rules. In Australia, SMS marketing is governed by the Spam Act 2003 and enforced by the Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA). The rules are clear and the penalties for breaking them are serious.</p>

<p><strong>Consent is mandatory.</strong> You must have explicit consent before sending marketing messages via SMS. Someone booking an appointment with you does not automatically give you permission to send them promotional texts. You need a clear opt-in, and you need to keep a record of it.</p>

<p><strong>Identification is required.</strong> Every SMS you send must clearly identify your business. The recipient needs to know who the message is from. Use your business name as the sender ID whenever possible.</p>

<p><strong>Opt-out must be easy.</strong> Every marketing SMS needs to include a way for the recipient to unsubscribe. "Reply STOP to opt out" is the standard approach. When someone opts out, you must honour it immediately. No delays, no extra messages, no "are you sure?" follow-ups.</p>

<p><strong>Transactional messages are different.</strong> Appointment reminders, booking confirmations, and delivery notifications are not marketing. They are transactional messages, and they have different (less restrictive) rules. You can send these without marketing consent, as long as they are directly related to a service the customer has engaged with. But do not try to sneak promotional content into a transactional message. Regulators see through that quickly.</p>

