<h2>What to send via SMS (and what to leave for email)</h2>

<p>SMS is not a replacement for <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email</a>. It is a complement. Each channel has its strengths, and using them correctly means matching the message to the medium.</p>

<p><strong>Use SMS for:</strong></p>

<ul>
  <li>Appointment reminders and confirmations</li>
  <li>Time-sensitive promotions (flash sales, last-minute availability)</li>
  <li>Review requests with a direct link</li>
  <li>Short follow-ups after a service</li>
  <li>Booking confirmations and changes</li>
  <li>Quick check-ins with active leads</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Leave for email:</strong></p>

<ul>
  <li>Newsletters and longer content</li>
  <li>Detailed product or service information</li>
  <li>Welcome sequences with multiple pieces of content</li>
  <li>Invoices and detailed receipts</li>
  <li>Educational content and guides</li>
</ul>

<p>The rule of thumb is simple. If the message is short, time-sensitive, and requires a quick action, use SMS. If it is longer, more detailed, or informational, use email.</p>

<h2>Timing your messages</h2>

<p>When you send an SMS matters almost as much as what you send. Get the timing wrong and even a good message will feel intrusive.</p>

<p>For <a href="/blog/reduce-no-shows">appointment reminders</a>, send the first one 24 hours before and a second one two hours before. This combination gives people time to reschedule if needed, while the second reminder catches the ones who forgot.</p>

<p>For promotional messages, business hours are safest. Between 10am and 7pm on weekdays tends to perform well. Avoid early mornings, late nights, and Sundays unless your audience has shown they are receptive at those times. Nobody wants a marketing text at 6am.</p>

<p>For review requests, send them one to two days after the service. Close enough that the experience is fresh, but not so immediate that it feels pushy.</p>

<h2>Templates you can use today</h2>

<p>Here are ready-to-use templates for the most common SMS marketing scenarios. Customise them with your business name and details.</p>

<p><strong>Appointment reminder:</strong><br>
"Hi [name], just a reminder about your appointment with [business] tomorrow at [time]. Reply C to confirm or call us on [number] to reschedule."</p>

<p><strong>Review request:</strong><br>
"Hi [name], thanks for visiting [business]. We would love to hear how it went. Leave a quick review here: [link]. It means a lot to us."</p>

<p><strong>Promotional offer:</strong><br>
"[Business] flash deal: [offer details] this [day] only. Book now: [link]. Reply STOP to opt out."</p>

<p><strong>Post-service follow-up:</strong><br>
"Hi [name], thanks for choosing [business]. We hope everything is going well. If you need anything, just reply to this message."</p>

<p><strong>Re-engagement:</strong><br>
"Hi [name], it has been a while since your last visit to [business]. We would love to see you again. Book your next appointment here: [link]. Reply STOP to opt out."</p>

<blockquote><p>The best SMS messages read like they came from a person, not a marketing department. Keep them short, warm, and useful.</p></blockquote>

